# Copy this to e2e_test/.env.pagekite and fill it in with your own pagekite kite.
#
# Native OIDC requires the token issuer to be reachable by the cluster over HTTPS with a
# real certificate. The extension serves plain HTTP inside the compose network, so we
# expose it through pagekite, which gives it a public HTTPS address. The kite the cluster
# fetches the issuer's keys from is:
#
#     ${PAGEKITE_CONTEXT:-environ}-${suffix}
#
# so with the values below the issuer is  https://environ-you.pagekite.me , and the API
# server's oidc-issuer-url (and the token's `iss`) is that. Make sure the kite name is one
# your pagekite account owns, and that kitesecret is its shared secret.
#
# The kite ${PAGEKITE_CONTEXT:-environ}-${suffix} is a *new* sub-kite, which pagekite only
# registers on first (interactive) use — so up.sh registers it for you through pagekite.net's
# API before starting the tunnel. That needs your account login (used only to fetch a
# short-lived credential); the kite's shared secret is set to kitesecret below.
#
# This file is git-ignored and is NOT removed by `down.sh --wipe` — it is yours to keep.

# Your kite domain suffix (everything after the ${PAGEKITE_CONTEXT}- prefix).
suffix=you.pagekite.me

# The shared secret pagekite authenticates the tunnel with.
kitesecret=replace-me

# Your pagekite.net account, so up.sh can register the sub-kite non-interactively.
pagekite_email=you@example.com
pagekite_password=your-pagekite-account-password
